IMPORT prelude

-- Police Force Act 2004 (Singapore) -- the powers and protections that reach members of the public: ss 24 to 27
-- (prosecution of officers, warrants, road barriers, attempted-suicide and medical-emergency powers, private
-- employment of police), s 64 (Commercial Affairs Officers) and s 65B(2) (forensic specialists cannot arrest).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/ (amendments
-- to Act 16 of 2024 and Act 43 of 2024). The deposit's arrangement of sections runs out of step with the body from
-- Part 12; the encoding follows the body's numbering.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 64 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository; the most cited section is s 64
-- (17 Acts), the Commercial Affairs Officers. SCOPED to what touches the public. Not encoded: the constitution,
-- engagement and discipline of the Force (Parts 2 and 3 Divs 2 and 3), committees of inquiry, overseas service, air
-- marshals, the Special Constabulary, Auxiliary Police Forces except s 86A, and the Police Fund.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Police Force Act 2004 -- powers`

§§ `24 -- officers and the ordinary law`

-- s 24(1): nothing prevents prosecuting a police officer under any other written law. (2): "No person who has been
-- acquitted by a court of any offence is to be tried on the same charge under this Act." (4): no pay accrues while
-- serving a sentence of imprisonment.
GIVEN `acquitted by a court of the charge` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may be tried on the same charge under this Act` IF
    NOT `acquitted by a court of the charge`

§§ `25 -- acting under a warrant`

-- s 25(1): where the defence is that the act was done in obedience to a warrant "purporting to be issued by any
-- competent authority", the court "is, upon production of the warrant containing the signature of such authority and
-- upon proof that the act complained of was done in obedience to such warrant, to enter judgment in favour of such
-- police officer". (2): no proof of signature unless the court has reason to doubt it.
-- (3): "Where it is proved that the signature on a WARRANT CARD is not genuine, judgment is nevertheless to be given in
-- favour of such police officer if ... the police officer believed on reasonable grounds that the signature was genuine."
--
-- Subsection (3) says "warrant CARD". The warrant card is the officer's own identification (s 8); the document the
-- section is about is the warrant. Read literally, (3) covers a forged signature on the officer's identity card and
-- not a forged signature on the warrant. The encoding follows the text: a forged WARRANT is not saved by (3).
DECLARE WarrantDefence HAS
    `the warrant was produced` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the act was proved to be done in obedience to it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the warrant's signature is proved not genuine` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the officer reasonably believed the signature genuine` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the forged signature was on the warrant card rather than the warrant` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the defence` IS A WarrantDefence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `judgment must be entered for the officer` IF
        `the defence`'s `the warrant was produced`
    AND `the defence`'s `the act was proved to be done in obedience to it`
    AND (    NOT `the defence`'s `the warrant's signature is proved not genuine`
         OR  (    `the defence`'s `the forged signature was on the warrant card rather than the warrant`
              AND `the defence`'s `the officer reasonably believed the signature genuine`))

§§ `26 -- road barriers`

-- s 26(1): any police officer may, if they consider it necessary for law and order or crime, put barriers across any
-- public road or place. (2): an officer may order a driver or rider "(a) to proceed towards the barrier and stop ...;
-- and (b) to remain in the vehicle and keep the vehicle stationary until permitted".
-- (4): a notice or sign warning of the barrier "constitutes and is to be treated ... as an order under subsection (2)
-- addressed by a police officer to any driver ... who OUGHT REASONABLY TO HAVE SEEN the notice or sign".
-- (5): failing to comply is an offence; (8)(a): "$10,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not exceeding 7 YEARS or ...
-- both". (6), (7), (8)(b): a PEDESTRIAN who fails to comply with a direction -- $2,500 or 3 months.
-- (9): defence, on the balance of probabilities, "that it was not reasonably practicable to do more than what was in
-- fact done to comply".
-- (10): arrest without warrant of a non-compliant driver or pedestrian, or one who refuses a name and Singapore
-- residential address, or gives "an address other than the full and correct residential address in Singapore".
-- (11): "No police officer shall be liable for any loss or damage to any vehicle, or for any injury to the driver or
-- rider or any other occupant ..., as a result of the driver or rider ... failing to obey".
--
-- A driver who does not see a roadblock sign that they ought reasonably to have seen is treated as having been ordered
-- to stop, and faces seven years. The only defence is that more was not reasonably practicable -- not that the sign
-- was missed. The occupants' injuries caused by a driver's failure to stop are not the police's liability, even for a
-- passenger who could do nothing.
DECLARE Road IS ONE OF
    `a driver or rider`
    `a pedestrian`

DECLARE BarrierFacts HAS
    `who` IS A Road
    `ordered by an officer in words, a hand signal or a prescribed signal` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a warning sign was displayed that the person ought reasonably to have seen` IS A BOOLEAN
    `complied` IS A BOOLEAN
    `not reasonably practicable to do more than was done` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the facts` IS A BarrierFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person was ordered to stop` IF
        `the facts`'s `ordered by an officer in words, a hand signal or a prescribed signal`
    OR  (    `the facts`'s `a warning sign was displayed that the person ought reasonably to have seen`
         AND CONSIDER `the facts`'s `who`
             WHEN `a driver or rider` THEN TRUE
             WHEN `a pedestrian` THEN FALSE)

GIVEN `the facts` IS A BarrierFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person commits the barrier offence` IF
        `the person was ordered to stop` `the facts`
    AND NOT `the facts`'s `complied`
    AND NOT `the facts`'s `not reasonably practicable to do more than was done`

-- In months.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A BarrierFacts
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment for the barrier offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the facts`'s `who`
    WHEN `a driver or rider` THEN 84
    WHEN `a pedestrian` THEN 3

GIVEN `the facts` IS A BarrierFacts
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for the barrier offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the facts`'s `who`
    WHEN `a driver or rider` THEN 10000
    WHEN `a pedestrian` THEN 2500

GIVEN `the injury resulted from the driver's failure to obey` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a police officer is liable for injury to an occupant` IF
    NOT `the injury resulted from the driver's failure to obey`

§§ `26A to 26D -- attempted suicide`

-- s 26A(1): ss 26AA to 26D apply "only where a police officer reasonably suspects that a person is about to or has
-- attempted to commit suicide and where the powers ... are reasonably necessary" to (a) prevent injury or death,
-- (b) facilitate a coroner's inquiry if death is caused, or (c) preserve evidence of a suspected arrestable offence.
-- s 26B: search for or of a person without warrant, and break in. s 26C: search for documents or things.
-- s 26D(1): "A police officer may seize, or prohibit the disposal of or dealing in, ANY PROPERTY." (2): an inspector may
-- order a financial institution to deliver property or freeze an account. (7), (8): a court may release property ONLY
-- if necessary for basic expenses, legal fees, extraordinary expenses, a prior lien or judgment, or a Singapore
-- company's day-to-day operations.
--
-- Attempted suicide is no longer an offence in Singapore (that comes from the Penal Code, which this row does not read;
-- the Act itself does not say). Yet s 26D lets the police freeze a person's bank account because they attempted
-- suicide, and the person can get money released only for the listed needs. The freezing order under (2)(b) is "for such
-- period as may be specified in the order" -- the Act sets no maximum.
DECLARE SuicideCase HAS
    `the officer reasonably suspects an imminent or past suicide attempt` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonably necessary to prevent injury or death` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonably necessary for a coroner's inquiry after a death` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonably necessary to preserve evidence of an arrestable offence` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the case` IS A SuicideCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the suicide powers are available` IF
        `the case`'s `the officer reasonably suspects an imminent or past suicide attempt`
    AND (    `the case`'s `reasonably necessary to prevent injury or death`
         OR  `the case`'s `reasonably necessary for a coroner's inquiry after a death`
         OR  `the case`'s `reasonably necessary to preserve evidence of an arrestable offence`)

DECLARE ReleasePurpose IS ONE OF
    `basic expenses such as food, rent and medicine`
    `legal fees`
    `holding or maintenance charges`
    `extraordinary expenses`
    `a lien or judgment from before the freeze`
    `a Singapore company's day-to-day operations`
    `a holiday`
    `investing elsewhere`

GIVEN `the purpose` IS A ReleasePurpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may release frozen property for the purpose` IF
    CONSIDER `the purpose`
    WHEN `a holiday` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `investing elsewhere` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act limits how long an account may be frozen` IF
    FALSE

-- s 26B(2A): a woman must be searched by a police officer who is a woman.

§§ `26E -- forced entry for a medical emergency`

-- s 26E(1): an officer may break in if they "(a) reasonably suspect[] that a person in any place requires assistance
-- because of any injury or the person's poor state of health; (b) [are] unable to gain entry ...; and (c) [are] of the
-- view that entry ... is necessary to protect the life, health or safety". (2): not where the officer reasonably
-- suspects a suicide attempt -- those cases go through s 26B instead.
DECLARE EmergencyEntry HAS
    `reasonably suspects someone inside needs help through injury or ill health` IS A BOOLEAN
    `cannot get in` IS A BOOLEAN
    `entry is necessary to protect life, health or safety` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonably suspects a suicide attempt` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the entry` IS AN EmergencyEntry
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may break in under section 26E` IF
        `the entry`'s `reasonably suspects someone inside needs help through injury or ill health`
    AND `the entry`'s `cannot get in`
    AND `the entry`'s `entry is necessary to protect life, health or safety`
    AND NOT `the entry`'s `reasonably suspects a suicide attempt`

§§ `27 -- hiring the police`

-- s 27(1): "Any person may apply to the Commissioner" to employ police officers for guarding persons or property "or
-- any other reason acceptable". (3): "The Government shall not be liable for any loss or damage to property, whether
-- owned by the applicant or by any other person, consequent on the employment of police officers".
GIVEN `the loss was to property` IS A BOOLEAN, `consequent on the private employment of police` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Government is liable for the loss` IF
    NOT (`the loss was to property` AND `consequent on the private employment of police`)

§§ `64, 65B -- Commercial Affairs Officers and forensic specialists`

-- s 64(2): a Commercial Affairs Officer "may investigate any suspected offence which appears to him or her to have been
-- committed under ANY written law". (3)(a): with all a police officer's investigation powers under the CPC, deemed an
-- officer not below the rank of inspector; (b): may arrest without warrant a person concerned in an arrestable offence,
-- in possession of suspected stolen property, or obstructing the officer. (5): "Apart from this section and section 25,
-- this Act does not apply to a Commercial Affairs Officer."
-- s 65B(2): the Commissioner "cannot authorise ... a forensic specialist to arrest any individual" -- though under
-- (3)(a)(vi) the specialist may detain and search an individual to stop interference with a crime scene.
--
-- s 64(2) is not limited to commercial offences. The power to investigate is "any suspected offence ... under any
-- written law".
DECLARE Officer IS ONE OF
    `a police officer`
    `a Commercial Affairs Officer`
    `a forensic specialist`

DECLARE ArrestGround IS ONE OF
    `concerned in an arrestable offence`
    `holding suspected stolen property`
    `obstructing the officer`
    `none`

GIVEN `the officer` IS AN Officer, `the ground` IS AN ArrestGround
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may arrest without warrant on the ground` IF
    CONSIDER `the officer`
    WHEN `a forensic specialist` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `the ground`
        WHEN `none` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the offence is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Commercial Affairs Officer may investigate the offence` IF
    TRUE

GIVEN `interfering with a crime scene` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a forensic specialist may detain and search the individual` IF
    `interfering with a crime scene`
